# Security conventions

## Server-only code

- Any module that touches D1 directly, verifies tokens, or otherwise should
  never run client-side (`lib/server/**`) should start with `import "server-only";`.
  This fails the build loudly if a future refactor accidentally imports it
  into a client component, instead of silently leaking server logic (or
  worse, secrets) into the client bundle.

## Auth & authorization

- Firebase ID tokens are verified via real JWKS signature checks
  (`lib/server/auth/firebase-token.ts`) — never decode-only. Keep it that way.
- User-scoped reads/writes (favorites, library, profile) must always key off
  the verified token's `uid`, never a client-supplied id. See
  `app/api/v1/me/**` for the pattern.
- When the checkout/payment flow gets a real write endpoint: recompute price
  server-side from `(routineSlug, planId)` against `lib/pricing.ts`/D1.
  **Never** accept a price/amount field from the client request body.

## SQL

- Every D1 query goes through `.prepare(sql).bind(...)` with `?` placeholders.
  Never string-interpolate a request-derived value into a query string.

## Dependencies

- Run `npm audit` periodically (or after adding a dependency) and patch
  high-severity findings promptly, even dev-only ones (dev/preview servers
  still proxy real requests through them).

## Headers

- Security headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, HSTS, Permissions-Policy,
  Referrer-Policy) live in `next.config.mjs`'s `headers()` — extend that
  array rather than adding a second, competing source of truth in
  `public/_headers`.
